# Explain why, not what

This is a learning database. A reader should understand **why a line exists** without leaving the file.

When writing or editing Java, add a short comment on non-obvious behavior: OS/Java APIs, invariants, error choices, and why this is *not* some other layer (WAL, transactions, catalog).

Do **not** comment getters, setters, or restatements of the next line (`i++ // increment i`).

Prefer one or two sentences above the block. Name the consequence (crash, data loss, security, layering).

```java
// BAD — restates the call
channel.force(true);

// GOOD — why this API, what the argument means, what this is not
// write() can return after data is only in the OS cache; a crash would lose it.
// force(true) requests a durable write of file contents and metadata (size).
// This is not a transaction commit or WAL — only "persist this file now."
channel.force(true);
```

Also comment: path/sandbox checks, why create-before-write, why offset vs whole-file I/O, why an exception is wrapped.

Keep comments accurate. If you change the code, update the comment in the same edit.
